  "completion": "Below is a “cheat‑sheet” of the most frequent pitfalls you’ll bump into when you start writing generic code in Java, together with concrete ways to sidestep them.  The list is ordered roughly from the most common to the more subtle/advanced mistakes, so you can skim the sections that apply to you and then dive deeper if needed.\n\n| # | Common Mistake | Why it’s bad | How to avoid / fix |\n|---|----------------|--------------|--------------------|\n| 1 | **Using raw types** – e.g. `List list = new ArrayList();` or `Map map = new HashMap();` | The compiler loses all type information. You get unchecked‑cast warnings and runtime `ClassCastException`’s. | **Never** expose or use raw types.  Always write `List<T>`, `Map<K, V>`.  If you must interact with legacy code, wrap it in a generic helper (`List<Object> list = Collections.unmodifiableList(rawList);`). |\n| 2 | **Mixing raw and generic types** – e.g. `List<Integer> ints = new ArrayList();` or `List raw = new ArrayList<String>();` | The raw type erases the generic type, so you lose safety on the other side. | Don’t mix.  Create a generic instance (`new ArrayList<Integer>()`) or a raw instance (`new ArrayList()`) but never combine them. |\n| 3 | **Unchecked casts** – `List<String> strings = (List<String>) rawList;` | The compiler can’t prove that the cast is safe.  It issues an `unchecked` warning and you might hit a `ClassCastException` later. | 1. **Never** cast raw collections.  2. If you absolutely need it, document with `@SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")` only when you’ve verified the type (e.g. from a factory method that guarantees the content). |\n| 4 | **Wildcard misuse** – confusing `? extends T` vs `? super T`.  | Many newbies use `? super` where `? extends` is required, or vice‑versa, leading to either “cannot add” errors or “cannot read” errors. | *Rule of thumb*:<br>• **Producer** (you read from it) → use `? extends T`.<br>• **Consumer** (you write into it) → use `? super T`.  See *PECS*: **Producer Extends, Consumer Super**. |\n| 5 | **Bounded type parameter misuse** – e.g. `<T extends Number & Comparable>`, missing the generic on `Comparable`. | The compiler will accept `<T extends Number & Comparable>` but you’ll get an “unchecked” warning when you call `compareTo`. | Always parameterise all bounded types: `<T extends Number & Comparable<? super T>>`. |\n| 6 | **Creating generic arrays** – `T[] arr = new T[10];` or `List<T>[] arr = new List<T>[10];` | Generic type information is erased, so the JVM cannot create an array of a non‑reifiable type.  It produces an “illegal generic array creation” compile‑time error or a runtime `ArrayStoreException`. | • Use `List<T> list = new ArrayList<>();` and then `list.toArray(new T[0])` with a cast. <br>• For arrays, keep a `Class<T>` reference and use `Array.newInstance(clazz, size)`. |\n| 7 | **Varargs + generics leading to heap pollution** – `@SafeVarargs void foo(T... ts)` in a generic type. | The compiler warns “possible heap pollution from varargs parameter”.  It’s a real risk if the varargs array is later used to store elements of a different type. | • Mark the method with `@SafeVarargs` **only** if you can guarantee safety (e.g., the method never stores the vararg array). <br>• Prefer `Collection<T>` over varargs. |\n| 8 | **Relying on instanceof with generics** – `if (obj instanceof List<String>)`. | `instanceof` works only on reifiable types; the check always succeeds for `List<?>`.  At runtime you can’t distinguish `List<Integer>` from `List<String>`. | Use a `Class<T>` parameter (e.g., `Class<T> type`) and test `type.isInstance(obj)` or pass a `TypeToken` (e.g., Guava’s). |\n| 9 | **Poor type inference** – the compiler can’t figure out `T` and falls back to `Object`. | Code becomes less type‑safe, or you need to explicitly declare the type variable, which is tedious. | • Supply the type explicitly if inference fails: `<String>myMethod(arg)`. <br>• Refactor the signature to reduce ambiguity (e.g., separate methods for different type ranges). |\n| 10 | **Wrong generic method overriding** – overriding `List<E>`’s `addAll(Collection<? extends E> c)` with a more specific signature like `addAll(Collection<String> c)` in a subclass. | The overriding method must be compatible.  Making it more specific breaks polymorphism. | Keep the exact signature (or a compatible one) in subclasses.  Don’t narrow the wildcard. |\n| 11 | **Static fields in generic classes** – `static T value;` inside a generic class. | `static` belongs to the *class* itself, not to the type parameter.  All type arguments share the same static field, leading to surprising bugs. | Don’t declare `static T` fields.  Use an external holder (`Map<Class<?>, T>`) if you need per‑type storage. |\n| 12 | **Using primitives as generic type arguments** – `List<int>` or `Map<int, String>`. | Java generics work only with reference types. | Wrap primitives in their wrapper classes (`Integer`, `Double`, etc.). |\n| 13 | **Ignoring compiler warnings** – turning off `-Xlint:unchecked` or swearing “I’ll deal with this later.” | The warnings often point to genuine bugs.  Ignoring them can hide subtle errors. | Keep `-Xlint:unchecked` enabled.  Investigate and fix every warning. |\n| 14 | **Reifying generic type in a static method** – `static <T> List<T> makeList(T t1, T t2) { return Arrays.asList(t1, t2); }` | The method’s type parameter is inferred from the arguments, but if you call it with raw types, the compiler may fall back to `Object`. | Use the diamond operator (`Arrays.asList(t1, t2)`) and let the compiler infer; or supply the type explicitly. |\n| 15 | **Complex nested generics** – `Map<List<Map<String, Integer>>, Set<Double>>` | Hard to read, hard to maintain, and error‑prone. | Use type aliases (via helper classes or interfaces) or split the data structure into several layers.  Prefer domain objects over raw nested generics. |\n\n---\n\n## Practical “Avoidance Checklist”\n\n| Category | Best‑Practice |\n|----------|---------------|\n| **API Design** | • Public methods should expose *generic* types, never raw. <br>• Document expected type bounds. |\n| **Implementation** | • Prefer the *diamond operator* (`new ArrayList<>()`). <br>• When you need a generic array, keep a `Class<T>` or use `List<T>` instead. |\n| **Wildcards** | • Use `? extends T` for read‑only, `? super T` for write‑only. <br>• Avoid “unbounded wildcards” (`List<?>`) unless the method really doesn’t care about the element type. |\n| **Suppressing Warnings** | • Never blanket `@SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")`. <br>• Target only the minimal code segment, explain why it’s safe. |\n| **Varargs** | • Mark with `@SafeVarargs` only when you can prove no heap pollution. <br>• Prefer `Collection<T>` over varargs. |\n| **Type Erasure** | • If you need runtime type info, pass a `Class<T>` (or `TypeToken`). <br>• Avoid `instanceof` checks against generic types. |\n| **Testing** | • Write unit tests that pass different concrete types to generic methods. <br>• Verify that the type safety holds at runtime (e.g., no `ClassCastException`). |\n\n---\n\n### Quick Reference – “What to Do / What to Avoid”\n\n| Situation | Do | Avoid |\n|-----------|----|-------|\n| **Creating a generic collection** | `List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();` | `List names = new ArrayList();` |\n| **Method that consumes a generic type** | `void addAll(Collection<? super T> c)` | `void addAll(Collection<T> c)` when callers might supply a `Collection<Number>` for `T=Double`. |\n| **Method that produces a generic type** | `Collection<? extends T> getValues()` | `Collection<T> getValues()` if callers might need a `List<Integer>` but you return a `Set<Integer>`. |\n| **Using generics with arrays** | `T[] arr = (T[]) Array.newInstance(clazz, n);` | `T[] arr = new T[n];` |\n| **Generic factory** | `<T> T create(Class<T> type)` | `<T> T create()` without `Class<T>` – you lose runtime type safety. |\n| **Suppressing unchecked** | `@SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")` *just before* the specific cast | Apply to the whole class or method. |\n| **Varargs + generics** | `@SafeVarargs static <T> void process(T... ts)` only if method doesn’t store `ts`. | `static <T> void process(T... ts)` without safety annotation if you risk heap pollution. |\n\n---\n\n## Wrap‑up\n\n*Generics in Java are powerful, but they’re a compile‑time mechanism.  Runtime type safety is lost due to type erasure, so you must be careful to keep the compiler’s help in check.*\n\n- **Never expose raw types** – always fully parameterise.\n- **Use wildcards appropriately** – follow the PECS principle.\n- **Avoid generic array creation** – use `List` or `Array.newInstance`.\n- **Treat unchecked warnings seriously** – investigate them or provide a safe suppression.\n- **Remember type erasure** – don’t expect runtime `instanceof` checks to work on generics.\n- **Prefer the diamond operator** and let the compiler infer as much as possible.\n\nWhen you keep these guidelines in mind, your generic code will be safer, clearer, and far less prone to subtle bugs. Happy coding!",
